# The native compiler-rt builtins of the hosted Linux oracle E7h (plan
# 5.17.2, 5.17.3, M25; R19). E7h links no libgcc, so a hosted program that
# calls a runtime helper takes it from the archive of msil-prog.py
# --linux-rtlib FILE (make-linux-sysroot.py --builtins writes
# <linux sysroot>/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libclang_rt.builtins-x86_64.a),
# which E7h links after the program's inputs and -lm and before -lc; E7 keeps
# libgcc_s.so.1. update-expected.py --linux-rtlib auto|none|PATH gives its
# hosted Linux oracle the same, and lit's msil_linux_rtlib (auto, none or a
# path) passes the archive to the hosted directories. The builds below use
# the stand-ins of prog/Inputs/prog-cc.py, which take E7h's link only in
# exactly the recipe's order and record the inputs in link order; the
# stand-in archive's member adds 64 to the program's exit status, so the
# status shows whether the archive was linked.
# (1) msil-prog.py E7h (--hosted with the E7 options) with --linux-rtlib FILE:
#     the hosted compiles, and the link with FILE between -lm and -lc, pinned
#     as linux-hosted-oracle.test pins E7h's flags; status 1+2+8+64.
# (2) Without --linux-rtlib, and with an empty --linux-rtlib= after one (it
#     cancels it): -lm -lc, status 11.
# (3) E7h's shared libraries (--shlibs): each library links its own copy of
#     FILE between -lm and -lc, as the program does (status 1 + the
#     library's 16+64 + 64); without FILE neither link names it (status 17).
# (4) E7 (no --hosted) is unchanged: ld.lld with the sysroot's libm.so.6,
#     libc.so.6 and libgcc_s.so.1 and no FILE, although FILE is given.
# (5) The driver recipe of a hosted Linux build (no E7 options, the Linux
#     host's link): FILE after NATIVE-LDFLAGS; without --hosted no FILE.
# (6) A FILE that does not exist is msil-prog.py's usage error (status 2,
#     nothing built) in the builds that use it, after E7h's own checks (a
#     missing glibc sysroot is reported instead); E7 and the driver recipe
#     without --hosted ignore it.
# (7) update-expected.py --os linux on a hosted test (a directory with the
#     marker of plan 5.17.3) with a PATH writes the transcript of the program
#     with the archive; --linux-rtlib none leaves it out, so --check fails;
#     auto finds the archive in --linux-sysroot and --check passes; auto
#     without an archive there fails the test (FAIL (native compile)) after a
#     line that names the file it tried and writes nothing; a PATH that names
#     no file is a usage error (status 2) that writes nothing.
# (8) lit (a nested lit with -a on Inputs/rtlib/nested, a hosted directory
#     and a plain one, for the Linux triple under the WSL runner, with the
#     synthetic sysroots of this test): under msil_native_oracle=required a
#     hosted directory without the archive is a configuration error (status
#     2) for auto (the default) and for none, and an explicit path that names
#     no file is one too; with the archive found by auto, or named by a path,
#     the hosted directory gets it in %{msil_linux_rtlib}, %{msil_e7_args}
#     and %msil_prog_build and the feature msil-linux-rtlib; without
#     msil_native_oracle=required the hosted directory loses its native
#     oracle, with a note, and the plain directory keeps it; a run of the
#     plain directory alone needs no archive.
# (9) lit and the MSIL triple's helper archive clang_rt.builtins (plan 5.14.3
#     A1; M25.e (1)): nested lits with the Windows triple, the local runner,
#     the obj pipeline at -O0 and msil_probe_cache= (empty: no probe cache is
#     read or written), whose clang (CLANG, which lit's use_clang honors) is
#     a hard link of %clang (a copy where the link fails) in a private
#     toolchain under %t, so that its resource directory, lib/clang/<version>
#     there, holds only what this test puts in it, whatever the build's
#     holds. With no archive there, lit notes the path it tried and names
#     build-builtins.py, and the test of Inputs/rtlib/noarchive, whose
#     program calls __divdc3, is a FAIL with msil-ld's undefined-symbol report
#     and N1's line (plan 5.14.2), never UNSUPPORTED (a missing archive is a
#     note, never a feature). With a stand-in file at A1's place (lit checks
#     only that it exists) lit notes 'helper archive: <path>'; that run lists
#     the tests (--show-tests) and runs none.
# (7) and (8) need WSL (the stand-in's Linux programs are shell scripts that
# update-expected.py runs under WSL; msil_runner=wsl needs a Windows host);
# (9) needs a Windows host and the built clang, msil-ld and llvm-ar.

# DEFINE: %{rt_cc} = "%python" "%S/prog/Inputs/prog-cc.py"
# DEFINE: %{rt_prog} = "%python" "%S/../../utils/msil-prog.py" --mode native --triple x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil --clang='%{rt_cc} --as cc' --ar='%{rt_cc} --as ar' --ld-lld='%{rt_cc} --as ld-lld' --linux-sysroot=%t/e7 --start=%S/../../utils/linux-oracle/start.c --glibc-sysroot=%t/glibc
# DEFINE: %{rt_cflags} = --cflags='-std=gnu17 -ffreestanding -Werror=return-type -I%S/../Inputs'
# DEFINE: %{rt_hosted_cflags} = --cflags='-std=gnu17 -Werror=return-type -I%S/../Inputs --sysroot=%t/glibc'
# DEFINE: %{rt_file} = %t/rt/libclang_rt.builtins-x86_64.a
# DEFINE: %{rt_ue} = %{harness_update_expected} --os linux %{msil_e7_args} --clang '%{rt_cc} --as cc' --ar '%{rt_cc} --as ar' --glibc-sysroot %t/glibc
# DEFINE: %{rt_lit} = %{harness_lit} -a --param msil_triple=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil --param msil_runner=wsl --param msil_glibc_sysroot=%t/glibc
# DEFINE: %{rt_tc_lit} = %{harness_lit} --param msil_probe_cache= --param msil_triple=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil --param msil_runner=local --param msil_linux_sysroot= --param msil_pipeline=obj --param msil_opt_levels=O0

